※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ A record of one field is a record like any other: made, handed to a call,
※ answered with, and a reference taken into it.
※
※ **One field is not one value.**  What a record is made of is asked of the type
※ and not counted -- a record of one field is made of parts and has one of them,
※ a `u32` is not made of parts at all -- and every place that chose between the
※ one-register path and the part-by-part one asks that question rather than
※ counting.  Before this the count was the question, so a record of one field was
※ read like a `u32` and the back end refused a record.
※
※ **Why it matters here.**  A descriptor is naturally one number, and a record
※ around it is what says which of the things that may be done with a number may be
※ done with this one.  A language that made a program add a second field to get
※ that would be charging for the abstraction.
※
※ Compare: **C**, where a structure of one member is a structure and the ABI says
※ so; **Rust**, whose newtype is exactly this and is the idiom for it; **Go** and
※ **Zig**, the same; **Haskell**, whose `newtype` goes further and costs nothing
※ at all by construction.

type Handle = fd : i32
type Pair = a : u32 ; b : u32
type Holder = only : Pair

fn open(n: i32) → Handle:
    Handle(.fd ← n)

fn number(h: Handle) → i32:
    h.fd

@[impure]
fn bump(at: &mut i32):
    at⌖ ← at⌖ + 1i32

fn wrapped(p: Pair) → Holder:
    Holder(.only ← p)

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    let h : mut Handle = open(7i32)
    if number(h) ≠ 7i32:
        1u6
    else:
        ※ a reference to the one field, written through
        bump(&h.fd)
        if h.fd ≠ 8i32:
            2u6
        elif number(open(3i32)) ≠ 3i32:
            3u6
        else:
            ※ and one holding a record, whose one part is two leaves
            let w : Holder = wrapped(Pair(.a ← 1u32, .b ← 2u32))
            if w.only.b ≠ 2u32: 4u6 else: 0u6
